<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lima</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as City of Kings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lima is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, on the central coast in the Rímac valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founded it in January 1535 as the City of Kings, and it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> and the centre of Spanish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, with the University of San Marcos from 1551. San Martín proclaimed Peruvian independence there in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lima.jpg" alt="Lima" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Felipe Ruiz Hoyos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas%C3%ADlica_Catedral_Metropolitana_de_Lima_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
